Docs only. Corrects the method section rather than leaving it overstating what isolation achieved.

1. TWO OF THE THREE HARNESSES DID NOT SET THE PYTHONPATH OVERRIDE. The whole-suite and per-batch
   attribution runs put the worktree's python/ ahead of the editable-install .pth; the two per-file
   subset harnesses (the 20 runpy candidates and the 125 test_arc_* files) did not. Found while
   writing this up. Their conclusions still stand, but for a different reason than stated: the
   canonical repo's 31,564 tracked files were sha256-hashed before and after those runs and showed
   only this session's deliberate edits. Isolation was the intended mechanism; the hash comparison
   is the one that actually carries the conclusion. Said plainly rather than left implied.

2. ISOLATION LEAKED THREE TIMES, WHICH IS ITSELF A RESULT. experiment_3734, experiment_2427 and
   experiment_3351 each appeared as modified in the CANONICAL repo while every test run was pointed
   at a /tmp worktree. All three restored and verified byte-identical against HEAD. Two are
   explained by the hardcoded absolute path finding. The third is NOT:
   scripts/experiment_3351_gatemate_latency_benchmark.py:9 resolves
   REPO_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1], which is correct and repo-relative. So at least
   one leak route remains unidentified. Anyone repeating this survey should treat "ran it in a
   worktree" as a mitigation and rely on the before/after hash of the canonical tree instead.

3. Coverage section gains two stated gaps: subprocess writers cannot be attributed by a CPython
   audit hook (three of batch 01's sixteen are in that class), and the unidentified leak route.

